change('Worklive validates a task, builds scoped context, reserves a run slot and persists the run before contacting the model. The model proposes a next step. A tool broker validates its arguments, permissions, ownership and remaining limits, then executes the tool and records the observed result. The next model step receives that result. The loop stops on completion criteria, a blocker, cancellation, a repeated failure or a budget limit.', '''Worklive validates a task, checks the selected CLI and subscription authentication, builds scoped context, reserves a run slot and persists the run before launching the official tool. That CLI owns model requests and its reasoning loop. Worklive consumes structured events and exposes approved browser, file and collaboration tools through a local MCP bridge. Its broker validates each Worklive action and records the result. The run stops on completion criteria, a blocker, cancellation, repeated failure or a limit. Built-in CLI capabilities require separate enforcement, as specified in section 10.''')
change('## Delegation contract\n', '''## Subscription and terminal execution contract
All AI work uses the selected official CLI: task reasoning, drafting, reviews, summaries, delegated subtasks and any AI-generated conversation labels. Worklive has no model HTTP client, embedded inference SDK, API-key setup or hidden cloud inference service. Browser networking, local tool messages, deterministic exports and office animation are not model calls. Ambient chatter remains scripted and costs no model usage.
The app launches an unmodified executable with explicit arguments and prompt input through standard input or the CLI’s supported protocol. A terminal is available for login and user inspection; structured process output drives the office. Do not scrape terminal paint or interpolate user prompts into shell command strings. Launching an approved CLI is distinct from granting its agent unrestricted shell access.
Codex supports ChatGPT sign-in, and non-interactive execution reuses saved CLI authentication. The initial adapter can use codex exec with JSON events and exact session IDs. These capabilities support a local integration; they do not establish entitlement to every model or unlimited unattended use. [S19] [S20]
Claude Code supports subscription sign-in and programmatic CLI output. Its print mode can emit structured streaming events and resume a named session. Do not choose bare mode for this requirement: it does not use subscription login. Console OAuth is a distinct API-billed route, so the presence of an OAuth login alone is insufficient. [S21] [S22]
**Integration conditions.** Anthropic permits hosting the unmodified Claude Code binary under its stated commercial conditions, with end users authenticating themselves. It also prohibits credential intermediation and routing subscription credentials on users’ behalf outside that boundary. Worklive’s proposed path is local user-owned CLI execution with the provider’s own sign-in. Do not extract tokens or implement a replacement Claude login. Confirm that the exact shipped orchestration and onboarding fit this boundary before external release; do not treat terminal wrapping alone as proof. Preserve the binary’s available authentication methods. [S23]
**Subscription preflight.** Worklive starts productive runs only when the official tool reports the intended subscription mode. If missing, API-billed or ambiguous, explain the state and let the user correct it in the official tool. Do not alter shared authentication, log the user out, strip authentication methods from a binary or edit global settings automatically. Use a controlled launch configuration and audit alternate endpoints, API credentials, profiles, hooks and provider overrides. Never print their secrets. An unsupported configuration produces a blocker, not a guessed billing route.
**Session ownership.** Key sessions by provider, project, agent and conversation. Resume an exact saved ID, never the most recent global session. Several agents may share one user subscription but have separate histories and working directories. Provider quotas are shared account limits; adding desks creates no extra allowance. No shared account pool or automatic account rotation.
**Changing provider.** Queue the change for the next run; never migrate an active process or silently switch after a failure. Carry approved project context, artifacts, task state and an explicit handoff into a fresh session. Raw provider sessions are not interchangeable. Keep earlier runs attributed to their original provider. Cross-provider collaboration passes scoped assignments and results through Worklive, not credentials or complete private transcripts.
## Delegation contract
''')
change('No autonomous creation of permanent agents.', 'No autonomous creation of permanent agents. Provider-native subagents must be disabled or accounted for within these same depth, permission and concurrency limits; an invisible nested team must not bypass them.')
change('The proposed default is two model runs across the workspace and six visible identities. More agents queue fairly, with explicit user priority. Enforce a configurable step limit, elapsed execution limit and token or spend allowance per task. Waiting for user input suspends active execution time while retaining the task’s history.\nWarn before exhaustion and reserve enough allowance to summarize partial results. Reserve estimated cost before dispatch when provider pricing and limits support it, then reconcile actual usage. When a CLI or provider does not expose cost, display “Cost unavailable” and enforce observable turn, time and concurrency limits. Do not promise an exact currency cap that the adapter cannot enforce.', '''The proposed default is two active CLI runs across the workspace and six visible identities, with a conservative one-run default per provider until concurrency is tested. More agents queue fairly. Enforce observable action, elapsed-time and concurrency limits; report tokens and remaining subscription allowance only when the CLI exposes them reliably. Waiting for input suspends active execution time. A provider limit pauses new dispatch for affected work without interrupting unrelated providers.
Retain partial files and recorded events even when no final model summary is possible. Worklive never enables overage, buys credits, selects API authentication or switches providers automatically. Subscription access does not guarantee zero additional charges if the user has enabled provider-side extra usage. Before promising included-allowance-only execution, verify whether that setting can be checked and enforced; otherwise label billing visibility unavailable. Do not present an API-equivalent token estimate as an actual subscription charge.''')
change('Use structured browser actions for navigation, observation, links, forms, scrolling, text extraction and screenshots.', 'Expose the same run-scoped Worklive browser tools to both CLIs through local MCP; both tools document MCP support. The existing browser worker remains the session owner. [S24] [S25]\nUse structured browser actions for navigation, observation, links, forms, scrolling, text extraction and screenshots.')
change('Role and personality describe behavior; capabilities grant authority. Enforce permissions in the tool broker outside the prompt. Grant selected input files, run output directories, browser scope and approved connectors independently. No arbitrary shell is exposed in the initial release. Deny-by-prefix shell rules are not an adequate sandbox for a later coding edition.', '''Role and personality describe behavior; capabilities grant authority. Enforce Worklive tool permissions in the broker outside the prompt. Grant selected input files, run output directories and browser scope independently. Official CLIs also have built-in shell, file, network, hook and plugin capabilities: MCP alone does not contain them. Restrict or disable unneeded capabilities using supported controls and an appropriate process sandbox. If an adapter cannot enforce the MVP scope, it fails the release gate. Never use permission-bypass flags. Deny-by-prefix shell rules are not an adequate sandbox.
Use an app-managed run directory and an explicitly reviewed configuration. Do not launch against arbitrary downloaded projects whose hooks or tools could execute during startup. Verify that built-in tools, inherited configuration and native subagents cannot evade browser ownership or file boundaries. Subscription credentials remain owned by the official CLI; a workspace boundary by itself cannot isolate an unsandboxed process.''')
change('Store provider credentials in the operating-system keychain and pass them only to the authorized adapter.', 'Let each official CLI store and refresh its own credentials through its supported mechanism. Worklive stores non-secret provider and session metadata; it neither reads token files nor copies credentials into prompts, subprocess arguments or its own database.')
change('Use a separately supervised browser and model worker where the browser automation and model libraries are mature. A Rust-based desktop application can legitimately include those workers.', 'Keep the existing supervised browser worker and add supervised official CLI processes. Rust manages process lifecycle and durable product state; the CLIs make model requests. No direct model SDK or separate model API worker is required.')
